# 2. What the tests are made of (no network, no real GitLab or AWS anywhere)
The test file builds four kinds of worlds: (a) the pure parts and the seed (the seed is built from the real files of this tree; its `npm test` is run with the real node in a temporary directory with no install, with an explicit environment so that a child `node --test` does not inherit NODE_TEST_CONTEXT; the metadata service is a stand-in for the request function, and for the hop-limit-1 case also a real server on 127.0.0.1); (b) real temporary git repositories holding the real seed, in which the commits the script would send to the Commits API are applied the way GitLab applies them, and the functions of the merge bot are imported and run on them (commitsInRange, judgeReviewCommit, localAfter, reviewCurrent, decide, checksFor, laneKeyOf, protectedTouched, PROTECTED_GATE_PATHS, GOVERNANCE_PATHS, isSessionId) and, new, the bot's own evaluate() over a local git remote with a fake GitLab (the reviewer's check bot-integration.mjs as a test: the same ten scenarios); (c) whole runs of run() against a fake GitLab built on scripts/eng/testing/fake-fetch.mjs (commits with trees, branches, merge requests, events, pipelines, jobs, comments), a scripted merge bot with the reasons in the order of mergeDecision, a fleet that boots machines (cap 24, auto-cancel of older pipelines, idle time, spot-like staggered boots), a fake aws runner whose autoscaling answers a test can script (a denial, a throttled call, a failure from call n on) and a virtual clock; (d) the seed self-test with the real child process, once on the real seed, once on a copy whose collab tooling drifted. globalThis.fetch is replaced by a function that throws for the duration of every whole run; the whole-run cases inject a quick stand-in for the self-test, and only the cases that are about it use the real one.
The sample outputs are produced by the same worlds: outputs/report-sample-simulated.txt, outputs/report-sample-a2-fail.txt, outputs/refusal-sample-asg-denied.txt, outputs/dry-run-sample.txt, outputs/refusal-sample.txt. They show the FORMAT of the report. They are a simulation and not the owner's run; the owner's run on the real system is what proves A1 to A4 and A8.

